{
"identity":"note-fidelity/missing/aspects.name-uniqueness-probe/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?/MISSING",
"source":"agent","catchable":True,
"evidence":(
"Real statement (in/real_mode.json, scenarios share-with-new-local-contact and inflected-locale-reads-profile, under frame ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?, inside the ActiveRecord::Base#save transaction): "
"SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"aspects\" WHERE LOWER(\"aspects\".\"name\") = LOWER(?) AND \"aspects\".\"id\" != ? AND \"aspects\".\"user_id\" = ? LIMIT ?. "
"Corpus: across all 417 dumps the only notes over \"aspects\" are `SELECT \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"user_id\" = $$(p0) AND \"aspects\".\"id\" = $$(p1) ORDER BY order_id ASC LIMIT 1` (FinderMethods#first, 414) and `SELECT \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (find_target, 42). No note anywhere names aspects.name, and none uses `id !=` (batch/notes.out). The policy IR has no such atom either. Verdict: MISSING. "
"MECHANISM, from source: Aspect has `validates_uniqueness_of :name, :scope => :user_id, :case_sensitive => false` (app/models/aspect.rb). share_with calls `contact.valid?` (connecting.rb:17) BEFORE `contact.aspects << aspect` (:21), so that validation does not reach the aspect; only `contact.save` (:22) does, through activerecord-5.2.4.3 autosave_association.rb:274-276 (`if new_record ... association.target`), i.e. only on the new-contact arm. In the corpus `save` is a declared target whose mock returns a seeded bool, so the validation chain inside save never runs and this read cannot be emitted on any path. "
"STATIC HALF: I ran checks.shape_check over in/policy.json and in/real_mode.json (batch/run_shape.py, venv venvs/queries_from_runs, output batch/shape_sqlglot.out). It reports this statement as `statement-shape/matched-under-another-target/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods.exists?`, not as a mismatch. It does so because the shapes keep predicate columns but drop operators: the real statement becomes {aspects.user_id, aspects.name, aspects.id}, and _subset_match returns True against the find_target note {aspects.id} (batch/shapes.out). That note reads ONE aspect by id. The real statement reads every OTHER aspect of the user with that name. Those row sets are disjoint, so this is not the same read under another frame."),
"consequence":(
"If true, the policy lacks a read the real request makes on the new-contact write path (the only arm that creates a contacts row). The real read covers all of the principal's aspects with a given name except the target one. The shipped views know aspects only by id (`aspects.id = ?`, alone or with user_id), so it is not covered, and an enforcer holding this policy would refuse the real request at its INSERT. This is finding 2, not an instrument limitation: a missing note is never declarable. Fixing it means changing the save mock so its validation reads are emitted (or emitting this probe explicitly). Do NOT fix it by editing the target list. "
"RULE P: the scenario that records it, `share-with-new-local-contact` in local_amc2/plan/real_mode_plan.json (key `scenarios`), already exists and already surfaces this statement through shape_check, though under the wrong label (see `check-fidelity/shape-match-ignores-predicate-operators`). Acceptance should be recorded as a 'caught' annotation on that scenario so it is never retired. No new config is needed. FALSIFIER: a corpus dump on the find_by-not-found arm that carries an exists? note over \"aspects\" with an aspects.name predicate, or a real recording of share-with-new-local-contact in which this statement is absent.")
},
{
"identity":"check-fidelity/shape-match-ignores-predicate-operators/statement-shape",
"source":"agent","catchable":False,
"evidence":(
"MEASURED in batch/ (batch/shapes.out): checks._shape_of reduces a WHERE clause to the SET OF COLUMN NAMES it mentions. It drops =, !=, IS NULL, IS NOT NULL and the LOWER() wrapper. Three real/note pairs from THIS endpoint therefore compare as identical or as covered when they are not the same predicate: "
"(a) real `... \"aspects\".\"id\" != ? AND \"aspects\".\"user_id\" = ? ...` vs note `SELECT \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"id\" = $$(p0)`. _subset_match returns True, so shape_check says matched-under-another-target. The row sets are disjoint. "
"(b) real `SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"contacts\" WHERE \"contacts\".\"person_id\" = ? AND \"contacts\".\"id\" != ? AND \"contacts\".\"user_id\" = ? LIMIT ?` (scenarios share-existing-receiving-contact and share-existing-nonreceiving-contact, frame FinderMethods#exists?) vs note `SELECT 1 AS one FROM \"contacts\" WHERE \"contacts\".\"person_id\" = $$(p0) AND \"contacts\".\"id\" IS NOT NULL AND \"contacts\".\"user_id\" = $$(p1) LIMIT 1` (340 notes, 0 notes with `id !=`). Their shapes are identical: {contacts.user_id, contacts.person_id, contacts.id}. "
"(c) note `... \"aspect_memberships\".\"contact_id\" IS NULL AND \"aspect_memberships\".\"aspect_id\" = $$(p0) ...` (12 notes, policy IR atom) has the same shape as real `... \"aspect_memberships\".\"contact_id\" = ? AND \"aspect_memberships\".\"aspect_id\" = ? ...`. "
"For (b), reading the source: Rails' UniquenessValidator adds `where.not(id: record.id_in_database)` for a persisted record (activerecord-5.2.4.3 validations/uniqueness.rb). The corpus's persisted contact is a `klass.allocate`d mock row (targets/core.rb symbolic_instance, ~1270-1300) whose id_in_database is never set. That yields `IS NOT NULL` with no bind. This is the same read in policy terms: the note's rows are a superset that adds only the found contact, which find_by already reads. But it is a mock defect, not an instrument limit, and no check sees it."),
"consequence":(
"If true, a passing shape_check does NOT establish that a note denotes the real read whenever the two differ only in predicate operator. This is the dangerous direction that _subset_match's own docstring exists to rule out. (a) shows it hiding a genuinely missing read as a frame-attribution issue. The defect is in the domain's shared comparator (checks.py/_shape_of over sql_shape.normalize), so every endpoint's shape_check verdict has the same blind spot. "
"CATCHABLE:FALSE, and why: no scenario, fixture row or must_stay_symbolic input can make an operator-blind comparator see an operator. The real statements for (a) and (b) are already in this drive's recording and the check still passes them. The fix is a code change: keep (column, operator) pairs and apply PRED-OP-DIFF semantics in the subset test. That change re-decides finished verdicts at other endpoints, so it goes to CONCRETE_FINDING_UNCATCHABLE. Escalated as ctl/ask/shape-match-operator-blind.json (class sweep-wide); no answer at submission. FALSIFIER: a _shape_of output that distinguishes `id = ?` from `id != ?`, or _subset_match returning False on pair (a).")
},
{
"identity":"note-fidelity/not-same-write/mock-dml-ignores-record-state",
"source":"agent","catchable":False,
"evidence":(
"Three measured differences between corpus writes and the real recording. All three come from the mocked ActiveRecord::Base#save/#save! (targets/endpoints/aspect_memberships_create.rb:333-368, delegating to the shared targets/core.rb dml_note, dml_run_pending_autosaves! and dml_stamp_owner_key!). The mock returns a seeded bool and does not apply ActiveRecord's post-save record state (batch/cooc.py, batch/recv.py). "
"(1) NEW CONTACT GETS NO PRIMARY KEY. Real (share-with-new-local-contact, frame ActiveRecord::Base#save): `INSERT INTO \"aspect_memberships\" (\"aspect_id\", \"contact_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)`. Corpus, all 12 new-contact dumps (e.g. dump_auth_json_dse0007.json): `INSERT INTO \"aspect_memberships\" (\"aspect_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES ($$(p0), '<concolic_now>', '<concolic_now>')`, which is PROJ-DIFF with contact_id absent. The same 12 dumps then record the controller's lookup (aspect_memberships_controller.rb:51) as `SELECT \"aspect_memberships\".* FROM \"aspect_memberships\" WHERE \"aspect_memberships\".\"contact_id\" IS NULL AND \"aspect_memberships\".\"aspect_id\" = $$(p0) ORDER BY \"aspect_memberships\".\"id\" ASC LIMIT 1`. The real form of that lookup is `... \"contact_id\" = ? AND \"aspect_id\" = ? ...` (recorded in share-existing-receiving-contact). The new-contact scenario raised Redis::CannotConnectError at defer_dispatch before reaching it, so its new-contact instance is argued from source, not recorded. The IS NULL form is a read atom in in/policy.json. "
"(2) PERSISTED MEMBERSHIP RE-INSERTED. All 64 persisted-contact write dumps record the membership INSERT twice: once under save! and again under save via the autosave pass. Real (share-existing-receiving-contact and share-existing-nonreceiving-contact) issues it ONCE, under ActiveRecord::Base#save!, and no INSERT under save. "
"(3) UPDATE ON AN UNCHANGED ROW. 28 dumps where find_by_1_receiving == True was taken (e.g. dump_auth_json_dse0016.json) record `UPDATE \"contacts\" SET \"updated_at\" = '<concolic_now>', \"receiving\" = true WHERE \"contacts\".\"id\" = $$(p0)`. The real share-existing-receiving-contact issues no UPDATE on contacts: its save transaction is BEGIN, two validation exists? reads, COMMIT. The endpoint's dirty wrapper (aspect_memberships_create.rb:499-507) marks a column dirty on any write, including `receiving = true` over an existing true. ActiveRecord does not."),
"consequence":(
"If true, the corpus's write-path notes are not the writes the request issues. It drops a column from one INSERT, doubles another, and invents an UPDATE on one arm. It also puts a read atom in the policy (`contact_id IS NULL`) for a lookup the real request issues as `contact_id = <the new contact's id>`. The shipped views are unaffected by the writes themselves, because the fold refuses write-terminated atoms (statement_differential reports all four write relations as not-in-views). The IS NULL read atom does reach the fold. Whether any view then covers the real new-contact lookup depends on how contact_id is bound in the rendered views, which I could not render here. "
"CATCHABLE:FALSE, and why: every real statement involved has a note of matching shape on SOME arm (the 64 persisted dumps carry `contact_id = $$(..)` and the full-column INSERT). shape_check's existential, per-target matching therefore cannot see a defect that is wrong only on a particular arm or only in multiplicity. No scenario or fixture changes that. Catching it needs pairing each real scenario with the corpus dumps on its path, which is a check change. The cause is in shared targets/core.rb, used by every write endpoint. Escalated as ctl/ask/mock-dml-record-state.json (class shared); no answer at submission. To RECORD the new-contact lookup for real, the rig would need a reachable Redis or Sidekiq fake, which is a rig change and not a scenario (the plan's `_why_scenario_2` explains why nothing stubs it). FALSIFIER: a corpus whose new-contact dumps carry contact_id in the membership INSERT, or a real recording showing a second membership INSERT or a contacts UPDATE on the receiving==true arm.")
},
